\begin{abstract}
We study the Diophantine equation
$y^{2}=(36n^{3}-19-12mn)^{2}-(2m)^{3}$ in integers $(n,m,y)$, $m\neq0$.
Starting from a table of $29$ known solution triples, an exhaustive
divisor-pair search over $1\le|m|\le1.387\times10^{9}$ (with $n$ unbounded)
together with a torsion-translate correspondence yields $18$ further
triples, for a total of $47$ triples ($94$ signed solutions), and proves
this list complete in the searched range. We establish: an intrinsic bound
$m\le24n^{2}$ for positive $m$, giving a complete classification of
positive-$m$ solutions for all $|n|\le7602$; a stratified completeness
result to $|m|\le10^{10}$ for unbalanced divisor pairs; an explicit order-$3$
torsion section whose translation action pairs six fibers' solutions and
generates the two largest known solutions ($|m|\approx3.4\times10^{11}$ and
$5.4\times10^{12}$) from modest ones; and exact Mordell--Weil ranks for
$21$ fibers, including one of rank~$3$.
\end{abstract}

\section{The equation and the trivial family}

We consider
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
y^{2}=(36n^{3}-19-12mn)^{2}-(2m)^{3},\qquad (n,m,y)\in\mathbb{Z}^{3}.
\end{equation}
Setting $m=0$ gives the trivial family $y=\pm(36n^{3}-19)$; throughout,
\emph{solution} means a triple with $m\neq0$. Since only $y^{2}$ appears,
solutions come in pairs $(n,m,\pm|y|)$; we list triples $(n,m,|y|)$.

