\section{Method and completeness}

Write $A=36n^{3}-19-12mn$, so \eqref{eq:main} reads $A^{2}-y^{2}=8m^{3}$,
equivalently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pair}
d'e'=2m^{3},\qquad d'=\tfrac{A-y}{2},\ e'=\tfrac{A+y}{2}.
\end{equation}
Conversely, every signed divisor pair $(d',e')$ of $2m^{3}$ determines
$A=d'+e'$, and a solution requires $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cubic}
36n^{3}-12mn-(A+19)=0,
\end{equation}
which forces $A\equiv5\pmod{12}$. Enumerating, for every $m$ in a range,
all divisor pairs of $2m^{3}$ (segmented-sieve factorization; pairs
generated multiplicatively in tandem without divisions; residues modulo
$12$ tracked through the recursion; integer roots of \eqref{eq:cubic}
located by floating Cardano followed by exact $128$-bit confirmation) is
therefore exhaustive over that $m$-range with $n$ unbounded.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:complete}
The table lists every solution of \eqref{eq:main} with
$1\le|m|\le1.387\times10^{9}$. Exactly three rows lie outside this window
--- $(798,-5378466956142)$, $(14709,-2616641560)$, and
$(-29317,-335124555576)$ --- and each is the torsion translate
(Section~\ref{sec:translate}) of an in-window row on the same fiber.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Stratified completeness to $10^{10}$]
The only solutions with $1\le|m|\le10^{10}$ whose divisor pair
\eqref{eq:pair} contains an element of absolute value $\le64$ are
$(1,-3,55)$ and $(798,-5586,18347596867)$. \textup{(}For fixed $d$,
$d\mid2m^{3}$ confines $m$ to the progression $q(d)\mathbb{Z}$ with
$q(d)=\prod_{p^{a}\parallel d}p^{\lceil(a-[p=2])/3\rceil}$, and
$e=2m^{3}/d=(2q^{3}/d)k^{3}$ requires no division; the stratum
$d_{\min}\le64$, $|m|\le10^{10}$ was enumerated exhaustively.\textup{)}
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Positive-$m$ classification]\label{cor:posm}
If $m>0$ then $8m^{3}=A^{2}-y^{2}\le(36|n|^{3}+12m|n|+19)^{2}$, whence
$m\le24n^{2}$ for $|n|\ge2$ and $m\le25$ for $|n|=1$ \textup{(}the cubic
threshold is the root $t\approx23.0002$ of
$t^{3}-18t^{2}-108t-162$\textup{)}. Since
$24\cdot7602^{2}<1.387\times10^{9}$, for every $|n|\le7602$ the table's
positive-$m$ entries are complete: they are the ten rows with
$n\in\{-4741,-921,-367,-54,75,114,309,790,909,1759\}$, $m>0$, and fibers
absent from this list have no positive-$m$ solutions at all.
\end{corollary}

\section{The torsion-translate correspondence}\label{sec:translate}

For fixed $n$, substituting $X=-2m$, $Y=y$, $c=36n^{3}-19$ turns
\eqref{eq:main} into the elliptic fibration
\begin{equation*}
E_{n}\colon\quad Y^{2}=X^{3}+36n^{2}X^{2}+12ncX+c^{2},
\end{equation*}
on which integer solutions are integral points with even $X$, and the
trivial family is the tautological section $T=(0,c)$.

\begin{proposition}
$T$ has exact order $3$ on every fiber; in particular
$\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\subseteq E_{n}(\mathbb{Q})_{\mathrm{tors}}$ for all
$n$, and the family's generic rank receives no contribution from $T$.
\end{proposition}

Translation by $T$ gives the explicit solution-to-solution map (for the
appropriate sign $\varepsilon\in\{\pm1\}$ of $y$)
\begin{equation}\label{eq:map}
m'=-\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\frac{\varepsilon y-c}{2m}\right)^{2}
-36n^{2}+2m\right],
\end{equation}
valid whenever the right side is a nonzero integer. The map \eqref{eq:map}
pairs the solutions on six fibers:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
fiber & translate pair $(m\leftrightarrow m')$\\
\midrule
$n=1$      & $-3\leftrightarrow-51$\\
$n=-54$    & $4749\leftrightarrow-10743$\\
$n=798$    & $-5586\leftrightarrow-5378466956142$\\
$n=909$    & $1826300\leftrightarrow-11607484$\\
$n=14709$  & $473559320\leftrightarrow-2616641560$\\
$n=-29317$ & $-1373577672\leftrightarrow-335124555576$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The two largest known solutions are thereby explained: the giant
$(798,-5378466956142)$, $|m|\approx5.4\times10^{12}$, is the
$T$-translate of the modest $(798,-5586)$; and
$(-29317,-335124555576)$, $|m|\approx3.4\times10^{11}$, was \emph{produced}
by applying \eqref{eq:map} to the sweep-discovered
$(-29317,-1373577672)$ the moment the latter was found. By contrast, the
solution pairs on $n=19$ and $n=-4$ are not translates: those points are
independent in the free part, consistent with
$\operatorname{rk}E_{19}=3$ and $\operatorname{rk}E_{-4}=2$.

\section{Mordell--Weil ranks}

Exact ranks by two-descent (\texttt{ellrank}, PARI/GP 2.15.4), with known
integral points supplied as hints:
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{rk}=1&:\quad
n=1,\,15,\,114,\,131,\,-234,\,-2271,\,-9925,\,11527,\,14709;\\
\operatorname{rk}=2&:\quad
n=-4,\,46,\,-54,\,-57,\,75,\,93,\,-4741,\,-6561,\,16531,\,17309,\,27949;\\
\operatorname{rk}=3&:\quad n=19,\ \text{with generators of $X$-coordinate }
2394,\ 7703436/1369,\ 2123383/2601.
\end{align*}
$E_{-29}$ remains unresolved with $\operatorname{rk}\in[1,3]$ at descent
effort $2$ (possible nontrivial $2$-part of Sha); the fibers
$n=-570,\dots,-1641,-970,-14362,-29317,18344$ and similar have not yet
been computed. Seven rank-$2$ fibers
($n=93,-4741,-6561,16531,17309,27949$, conditionally $-29$) possess a
provably existing second generator not yet found (beyond naive search to
height $10^{5}$); each is a target for $4$-descent and, via the group law,
a potential source of further large solutions.

\begin{remark}[Empirical structure of the solution set]
No solution-bearing fiber tested has rank $0$. Of the $18$ new solutions,
$14$ were found by the uniform-in-$m$ sweep and $4$ by combining the
sweep with \eqref{eq:map} or pointing it out directly. Among distinct
fibers first discovered at $|m|>10^{8}$, seven of ten have $|n|>9000$ ---
a pronounced large-$|n|$ bias invisible to fixed-$n$ searches --- yet the
recent finds $n=-970$ and $n=-234$ show the small-$|n|$ fibers continue
producing at larger $|m|$. The known range of $n$ grew from
$[-1641,\,5118]$ to $[-29317,\,27949]$.
\end{remark}

\section{Software}

All computations are reproducible with the accompanying programs:
\texttt{divisor\_sweep.c} (exhaustive in $m$, $n$ unbounded; the
$|m|\le1.387\times10^{9}$ certification), \texttt{small\_cofactor\_sweep.c}
(stratified sweep, the $10^{10}$ proposition),
\texttt{run\_sweep.sh} (parallel driver), and
\texttt{verify\_solutions.py} (exact verification of the table and of any
new hits). Extending Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} costs
$\sum_{|m|\le M}\tau(2m^{3})$ divisor pairs at roughly $20$\,ns each
($\approx50$ core-hours for $M=10^{10}$), parallelizing trivially over
$m$-ranges; Corollary~\ref{cor:posm} reaches $|n|\le10^{5}$ at
$M=2.4\times10^{11}$. The negative-$m$ side of any fixed fiber requires
per-curve integral-point methods (e.g.\ Magma's \texttt{IntegralPoints}),
to which the rank table above is the natural input.

